::JOUR_U01_L04_GP_Q01::MLA.JOUR.MED.08
Which visual choice best supports accurate journalism? {
~Cropping a photo so it hides important context#Incorrect. Hiding context can mislead the audience.
=Using a clear photo, accurate caption, and layout that matches the story's importance#Correct. Strong journalism design supports accurate understanding.
~Making a small event look like a crisis with giant design#Incorrect. Exaggerating importance can mislead readers.
~Using a chart scale that distorts the numbers#Incorrect. Distorted chart scales weaken accuracy.
}

::JOUR_U01_L04_GP_Q02::MLA.JOUR.DIG.03
Why can a tight photo crop create an accuracy problem? {
~It always makes the image unreadable.#Incorrect. A tight crop can be clear but still misleading.
~It means no caption is needed.#Incorrect. Captions are still useful.
=It may leave out context that changes what the audience believes.#Correct. Visual accuracy depends on context as well as image quality.
~It proves the photo was edited illegally.#Incorrect. Cropping is not automatically unethical, but it must be accurate.
}

::JOUR_U01_L04_GP_Q03::MLA.JOUR.ETH.01
What should a caption do for a news photo? {
~Replace the story with the writer's opinion#Incorrect. A caption should provide accurate context, not unsupported opinion.
~Hide who or what is shown#Incorrect. A caption should clarify the image.
~Use dramatic language that changes the facts#Incorrect. Captions should not distort information.
=Identify the image accurately and connect it to the story context#Correct. This follows format rules for quality visual journalism.
}

::JOUR_U01_L04_GP_Q04::MLA.JOUR.MED.08
Which design trend reflects mobile audience habits? {
=Readable vertical layouts with short headlines and clear image cards#Correct. These choices fit how many audiences read on phones.
~Tiny text designed only for printed posters#Incorrect. Mobile readers need readable layouts.
~Charts with no labels#Incorrect. Labels help readers understand information.
~Photos with no captions because phones are smaller#Incorrect. Smaller screens still need clear context.
}

::JOUR_U01_L04_GP_Q05::MLA.JOUR.MED.08
What is the best test for a journalism design choice? {
~Does it look dramatic even if it misleads readers?#Incorrect. Drama cannot replace accuracy.
~Does it hide the facts so readers click more?#Incorrect. Hiding facts damages trust.
=Does it help the audience understand accurate information in the chosen format?#Correct. Journalism design should support accuracy, clarity, and format purpose.
~Does it make every story look equally urgent?#Incorrect. Design should match story importance.
}
